(mo-definition GO--0019235 "&quot;The series of events required for an organism to receive a slow pain stimulus, convert it to a molecular signal, and recognize and characterize the signal. Slow pain is often subjectively described as an aching or throbbing pain; in humans, the signals from a slow pain stimulus are perceived and relayed along unmyelinated C fibers to the central nervous system, reaching their target in about 1 second. Slow pain is often associated with tissue destruction.&quot; [http://www.people.vcu.edu/~mikuleck/ssspain/, http://www.spine-health.com/]")

